# Cloudflare Pages routing — app.premiselabs.co (tortoise-dashboard project).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (#3523)
# ---------------------------
# With no top-level 404.html, Cloudflare Pages assumes a single-page app and
# serves the root document for EVERY unmatched path — /admin and
# /nonsense-probe-xyz both returned 200 and rendered the dashboard overview,
# silently discarding the address the visitor asked for. 404.html makes an
# unknown path an honest 404; the rules below keep serving the app for the
# pathnames the app actually owns, and send /admin to the console it means.
#
# The dashboard is hash-routed (src/main.jsx reads location.hash; the tabs are
# #/overview, #/keys, …), so the app owns exactly three pathnames:
#
#   /         the app itself — served by the index.html asset
#   /welcome  in-app onboarding / first-run wizard (#1287, #1566)
#   /team     Stripe checkout + billing-portal return — the URLs
#             tortoise/hosted_api.py builds as _billing_default_success_url()
#             and _billing_default_portal_return()
#
# Everything else is not an app route and 404s. welcome.html (tortoise host)
# client-redirects into /welcome, and Stripe returns to
# /team?session_id=…&new_org=… — both must keep their URL AND query string, so
# they are 200-rewritten to the app document rather than redirected (a
# redirect would rewrite the URL the SPA branches on, and the ?session_id=
# handoff is read from location.search).

# Valid app pathnames → the app document. 200 = serve it at this URL.
#
# Destination is `/`, NOT `/index.html`: Pages normalizes a `.html` destination
# with its own redirect, so `/index.html` turns this 200 rewrite into a 308 to
# `/` — silently dropping the `/welcome` pathname that welcome mode keys on
# (and for /team, replacing the 200 with a redirect).
# `wrangler pages dev` flags that as an infinite loop and ignores the rule.
#
# Static rules precede any dynamic rule below (Pages applies the top-most
# match).
/team      /  200
/team/     /  200

# NO `/welcome` RULE — deliberately removed in the #4104 x #4006 merge.
#
# main added `/welcome / 200` for the in-app first-run wizard (#1287, #1566),
# which was correct when the app project had no `welcome.ts`. #4171 MOVED
# `functions/welcome.ts` onto this origin, and a matching FUNCTION beats
# `_redirects` on inbound routing — so the rewrite stopped serving the wizard
# and started actively breaking the recovery landing: `welcome.ts` serves the
# reset panel by calling `env.ASSETS.fetch(...)`, that call re-enters the ASSET
# router (where `_redirects` DOES apply), and `/welcome / 200` answered with the
# SPA document instead of `welcome.html`. The panel silently disappeared — the
# served page had no `#reset-panel` in it.
#
# `welcome.ts` is now the single owner of `/welcome` and decides on the server
# (signed in → app root; `?reset=1` → the panel; no session → `/auth`).
# Re-adding a rule here re-introduces the collision.

# /admin genuinely means the console — and #4171 MOVED the console to THIS
# origin (app.premiselabs.co).
#
# REMOVED here (#4104 x #4006 merge): these three rules sent
# `app.premiselabs.co/admin` -> `tortoise.premiselabs.co/admin`, the OPPOSITE of
# what `website/functions/_middleware.ts` does (`tortoise.*/admin` ->
# `APP_ORIGIN/admin`).
#
# They were also DEAD CODE, and this is the part worth knowing before re-adding
# anything here: Cloudflare Pages runs a matching FUNCTION before `_redirects`,
# so `functions/admin/[[path]].ts` served /admin and these rules never fired —
# verified on the real runtime (`wrangler pages dev dist`: `/admin` with no
# cookie answers 302 `/auth?next=%2Fadmin&stale=1` from the gate, not 301 to
# tortoise). They were removed for being contradictory, not because they were
# live: leaving them in would make the file disagree with the architecture and
# would go live the moment the gate stops matching.
